# "Motion" in S2 is motion evident to the senses, while S3–S5 take it as any passage from potentiality to actuality.
# I read S2's motion as a case of that general sense, not a second sense.
# "First mover" could mean the first member of a series in order (P7) or the primary cause on which all others depend (P9, P10).
# I count it as one sense because every statement adds "put in motion by no other," and the hand-and-staff example in S12 treats the series as one of present dependence throughout.
